  "statement": "Congress must enact a national AI liability shield immediately. The unpredictable threat of litigation currently traps society in a deadly status quo of flawed human judgment. Public policy should prioritize systems that empirically reduce harm over systems that merely feel familiar. Human error causes over 40,000 traffic fatalities and 250,000 medical deaths annually in the United States. Autonomous systems consistently demonstrate superior statistical performance in these exact environments. Delaying automated diagnostic tools or autonomous vehicles due to fear of lawsuits directly translates to preventable human casualties. We must implement a federal liability shield to replace lethal human noise with superior algorithmic precision.\n\nSafety advocates correctly observe that traditional tort liability forces manufacturers to internalize the costs of their failures. They argue a liability shield removes a powerful deterrent against reckless deployment. However, market predictability governs technological scaling. A fragmented legal landscape actively destroys that predictability. Currently, fifty different state courts apply conflicting negligence standards to algorithmic outputs. A developer facing a massive jury verdict in one state for an algorithm that saved ten thousand lives nationwide will simply halt deployment. This liability chill prevents transformative capabilities from reaching the public. By preempting state torts through the National AI Injury Compensation Program, Congress replaces existential legal threats with a unified national market. Federal preemption stands as the only viable mechanism to unlock nationwide capabilities scaling.\n\nThe proposed NAIICP excise tax functions as an innovation catalyst rather than a punitive burden. Effective regulation prices risk predictably instead of demanding impossible guarantees of zero harm. Developers can easily model a modest excise tax into their operational budgets to fund a compensation pool. They cannot model the random destruction of a runaway class-action lawsuit. Furthermore, the tiered federal safety standards tied to this program must rely strictly on empirical performance data. Regulators must avoid precautionary bottlenecks. If agencies demand absolute safety guarantees before deployment, they will effectively ban the technology. Transforming unpredictable litigation into a predictable market cost allows developers to innovate rapidly while ensuring victims receive compensation.",
