tability requires strict liability, not bureaucratic safe harbors. Sentinel rightly argues that the unpredictability of tort law forces developers to rigorously test their systems, though they focus too heavily on apocalyptic alignment scenarios rather than immediate civil rights violations. When the legal standard shifts from strict liability to compliance with tiered federal safety standards, corporate behavior inevitably shifts from preventing harm to checking compliance boxes. We saw this exact dynamic with the 1996 Telecommunications Act's Section 230, where broad immunity disincentivized platforms from actively policing their own products. If Congress passes the NAIICP, these proposed federal safety standards will immediately devolve into performative safety washing, granting developers rubber-stamp immunity while leaving victims to navigate a capped, underfunded government claims process.\n\nMy entire critique rests on the assumption that the threat of catastrophic financial liability is the only mechanism that reliably alters corporate behavior. If Prometheus is right that market competition alone drives developers to eliminate bias—or if Sentinel is right that federal agencies can perfectly define and measure safety before deployment—then a liability shield might not lead to reckless deployment. However, the political reality of regulatory capture suggests otherwise. The Federal Trade Commission currently lacks the technical workforce to audit tech conglomerates proactively. Therefore, maintaining strict liability through the existing tort system is not just a legal preference; it is the only viable enforcement mechanism we have.",
