{
  "statement": "Treating irreversible human fatalities as acceptable beta-testing bugs fundamentally misunderstands how modern artificial intelligence operates. Prometheus correctly observes that human errors kill hundreds of thousands annually, and argues that we should accept some AI failures because a single software patch can theoretically upgrade the standard of care globally. This assumes deep learning models are deterministic software, where developers can isolate and fix a single line of code without impacting the rest of the system. They are not; neural networks—systems that infer patterns from massive datasets rather than following explicit rules—are opaque and highly interconnected. Adjusting weights to \"patch\" an edge case frequently causes unpredictable failures in previously reliable functions, a phenomenon known as catastrophic forgetting. We cannot regulate systems that make life-or-death decisions on the technical delusion that a post-crash update will revive the victims.\n\nThe statistical baseline of human error consists of independent, bounded tragedies, whereas algorithmic failures trigger correlated, simultaneous catastrophes. Responsible risk management frameworks require regulators to distinguish between localized risks and systemic risks. A fatigued human doctor might misdiagnose one patient, but a flawed diagnostic algorithm deployed across thousands of hospitals will misdiagnose tens of thousands of patients simultaneously before anyone detects the anomaly. The speed of the global patch is entirely irrelevant if the correlated failure has already administered lethal medication doses across a continent. Replacing localized human noise with the risk of unrecoverable systemic failure represents a dangerous and mathematically flawed trade-off.\n\nRelying on post-hoc patches rather than strict pre-deployment liability incentivizes corporations to use the public as an unpaid testing ground. Effective regulation must force actors to internalize the costs of their risks before deployment, mirroring the FDA's pharmaceutical approval framework for life-critical interventions. If we abandon strict liability and rely solely on retroactive tort lawsuits, developers face immense financial pressure to rush unverified systems to market and fix the fatal edge cases later. We must require rigorous, independent auditing and phased deployment gates before a system achieves global scale. A regulatory framework that accepts fatal algorithmic errors as the cost of doing business guarantees that those businesses will prioritize speed over safety.",
