{
  "statement": "Relying on uncapped tort liability to regulate artificial intelligence guarantees a future defined by the deadly, unpatchable baseline of human error. Cassandra rightly points out that without financial pain, corporations often ignore civil rights violations and algorithmic bias affecting marginalized groups. However, effective regulation requires systematic, predictable iteration rather than random penalization. Tort law operates as a reactive lottery, forcing society to wait for harm to occur and enriching lawyers while doing nothing to proactively audit the models. The National AI Injury Compensation Program replaces this chaotic threat with structured accountability, requiring continuous, data-driven auditing for bias as a strict condition of the liability shield. We must abandon the fiction that lawsuit lotteries are an effective regulatory mechanism for systemic bias.\n\nThe correlated nature of algorithmic systems is their greatest safety feature, not their fatal flaw. Sentinel correctly observes that a single algorithmic flaw can trigger simultaneous, widespread failures across thousands of deployments, unlike localized human mistakes. Safety frameworks, however, must evaluate the net speed of system-wide correction alongside the initial scale of failure. Because software scales, fixes scale instantly across the entire ecosystem. When an autonomous vehicle encounters a novel edge case and fails, developers push a patch that updates the entire global fleet overnight. Human error remains permanent and unpatchable; regulators cannot download a software update to prevent thousands of different human drivers from driving drunk tonight. Treating correlated algorithmic architecture as a liability ignores the reality that instantaneous, global deployment of fixes makes these systems fundamentally safer than the fragmented human baseline.\n\nDelaying artificial intelligence deployment to preserve traditional liability mechanisms imposes a massive, measurable cost in human lives. Policy decisions must weigh the hypothetical risks of new technology against the documented, ongoing casualties of the status quo. Nearly 40,000 Americans die in traffic accidents annually due to human error, and millions face documented bias in human-led medical and financial decisions. Uncapped liability threatens to bankrupt companies for a single autonomous failure, which perversely incentivizes corporations to withhold safer autonomous systems while society silently accepts thousands of human-caused tragedies daily. The compensation program provides a predictable path to deploy systems that are empirically safer than humans right now. We cannot let the unpredictable threat of litigation trap society in a deadly status quo when statistically superior autonomous systems exist today.",
